    "['ipv6 unicast-routing', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-options rib inet6.0 static', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set routing-options rib inet6.0 static', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset routing-options rib inet6.0 static\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you're using Junos versions that support the more direct equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-options router-advertisement interface all\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent in Juniper for enabling IPv6 routing is more complex than a single command. The first option creates the IPv6 routing table, while the second enables router advertisements which implicitly enables IPv6 routing. The most precise functional equivalent would be the first command.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-options rib inet6.0 static'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-options router-advertisement interface all', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set routing-options router-advertisement interface all', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset routing-options rib inet6.0 static\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you're using Junos versions that support the more direct equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-options router-advertisement interface all\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent in Juniper for enabling IPv6 routing is more complex than a single command. The first option creates the IPv6 routing table, while the second enables router advertisements which implicitly enables IPv6 routing. The most precise functional equivalent would be the first command.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-options router-advertisement interface all'}]]": 1,
