    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` address family under the interface's unit.\\n- The equivalent of Cisco's `ipv6 enable` is to configure `family inet6` under the interface unit in Juniper.\\n- Since the original configuration shows IPv6 being enabled on two interfaces (GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet1/0/4), both need to be configured in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` address family under the interface's unit.\\n- The equivalent of Cisco's `ipv6 enable` is to configure `family inet6` under the interface unit in Juniper.\\n- Since the original configuration shows IPv6 being enabled on two interfaces (GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet1/0/4), both need to be configured in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper command for the Cisco `ipv6 address` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- `ge-1/0/0` is the Juniper equivalent of Cisco's GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\n- `unit 0` is typically used as the default logical unit in Juniper\\n- `family inet6` specifies IPv6 configuration (equivalent to Cisco's `ipv6`)\\n- The address format remains the same (2001:db8:3::1/64)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 router [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name>', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface <interface-name>\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would need to specify the actual interface name (e.g., \"ge-1/0/0.0\" or \"ge-1/0/4.0\") when applying this command, unlike Cisco where it\\'s configured under the interface context. The exact interface naming might vary based on Juniper platform.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name>'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces <interface-name> family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces <interface-name> family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0` or `ge-1/0/4`). The `ipv6 enable` command in Cisco simply enables IPv6 processing on an interface, which in Juniper is achieved by configuring the `inet6` address family on the interface.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces <interface-name> family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:14::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for the Cisco `ipv6 address` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:14::1/64\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- `ge-1/0/4` is Juniper's equivalent interface naming for GigabitEthernet1/0/4\\n- `unit 0` is required in Juniper for logical interfaces (default unit)\\n- `family inet6` specifies IPv6 addressing (equivalent to Cisco's `ipv6`)\\n- The address format remains the same (2001:db8:14::1/64)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/4 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:14::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 router [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name>', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface <interface-name>\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would need to specify the actual interface name (e.g., \"ge-1/0/0.0\" or \"ge-1/0/4.0\") when applying this command. The Cisco \"ipv6 router isis\" command enables IS-IS IPv6 routing on an interface, which is equivalent to Juniper\\'s IS-IS protocol interface configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name>'}]]": 1,
